6:2026-09-15: the map AST is the Universal AST's canonical representation that gets evaluated by the ast-walker (a LISP using maps, not a LISP using vectors instead of lists). However, the Universal AST is converted into flat per-node tuple rows (`[id tag & slots]`) that act like bytecode, preserving everything in the Universal AST so that the semantic VM can execute it quickly because of the linearization of the AST into tuples (the VM lowers these rows into an instruction vector for execution). These rows are what is content-hashed, stored, streamed, and named — there is no nested tuple tree (ruling 1 of 2026-09-14,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:8:provenance-free; `dao.space.query/q` runs over the rows directly; all `yin.vm` evaluators are `dao.stream` observers that load rows to evaluate code; concurrently, a dedicated AST indexer observes the same stream and maintains the **row relation** of §6.1 (the `$ast` relation `q` runs over) from the rows it observes. `dao.jing` is the content store the rows already live in; it is not the indexer's output; code on a stream is rows end to end; `t` and `m` live in a ledger of\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:47:|                |                                | per-node tuple rows (§2) and        | id; a tree is named| (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | predecessor address inside     |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:51:| **Refs**       | [e :yin/code                   | Datoms `[e a v t m]` whose `v` is a | the datom itself,  | the ledger relation      | append-only; the hash chain is |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:64:terms named by hash — refs are `name → hash` entries with history, and a projection is a\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:67:**Where the semantic layer lives.** The map AST — a tag plus named fields,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:75:The third layer is named for its job, querying; its members are the\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:89:| naming                 | `[name :yin/code addr]` says what a name currently means; a name is mutable and retractable, content is not                        |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:107:the program is; datoms say which program we mean, where it came from, and\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:123:- **No datom-projection tempid is ever named by a ref, a ledger row, a\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:145:node is a map of its tag and its named fields:\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:156:The fields are what the walker dispatches on today (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:333-470`); the `:lambda` arm already binds `params` by name, and the `:variable` arm already resolves through `resolve-var`'s env → store → primitives → modules fallthrough (`ast_walker.cljc:361-363`; the semantic VM's `:var` opcode does the same at `semantic.cljc:256-259`) — this design keeps that fallthrough rather than splitting free and bound references into separate node types (§4.5). Separately, closure application (`ast_walker.cljc:191`, and its hot-path copies at `:511`/`:553`; `semantic.cljc:200`) still binds arguments with `(zipmap params args)`, which leaves missing parameter names unbound rather than bound to `nil` — that binding change belongs to the application arm and the semantic VM's apply, not to `:lambda`.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:236:|         |                                  | numbers are all portable keys today. `key` is a named kind rather than `data` only so that the same definition is cited by|\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:307:`:vm/store-update` is not a tag (§3.1). `:yin/macro-expand` is not a tag:\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:314:codec emits it as `:yin/value` (`v2.cljc:449`). The `:vm/resume` value slot\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:364:  `:variable name`), since this design preserves named variables across\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:367:  lambdas (`clojure.cljc:436-438`) and the codec persists `:yin/macro?`\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:373:- **`:yin/root`.** A tree is its own root; the batch or the ref that names\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:394:  `:yin/source-batch` of `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1), and the **member index**\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:402:  `:yin/source-medium` and `:yin/batch-token` in the batch envelope. A retry\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:481:The orchestrator's alternative, a `:fn` slot restricted to a named\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:538:root row recovers the entire tree, and every named payoff below names\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:610:every node of every tree is named, shareable, and queryable the moment it\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:632:Because this design retains named variables in the canonical tuple (§2, §5.1),\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:783:ruling clarified the AST level. To perfectly preserve program semantics across boundaries, both the Universal Map AST and the Semantic Tuples strictly retain named variables. Therefore, `\"v2\"` everywhere in this document means the Named Variable grammar. This design otherwise adds\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:989:`:yin/name`; joining refs to code is a join on the address value:\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:992:;; every call site, in the tree currently named `my.ns/f`, whose operator is `+`\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:995: :where [$refs ?e :yin/name my.ns/f]          ; bind the name entity\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:996:        [$refs ?e :yin/code ?root]            ; 3-slot, fast path, current view\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1009:preorder and one extra fact `[e :yin/address addr]` per node so the\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1026:and the only durable name in it is the `:yin/address` value.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1052:Datom-batch loading is the projection path: a batch of `:yin/*` datoms is\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1054:loaded as above; a batch of `:yin.code/*` datoms is decoded to a vector\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"The codebase has no existing Yang-to-durable-named-segment function: Yang returns AST maps, while REPL/encoder streams are runtime ingress. I’m treating that as a key boundary condition and will clear an explicit composition adapter rather than smuggling projection into the emitter or runtime encoder."}}
