rogram streams and `yin.vm.encoder` are runtime ingress paths, not a durable projected-segment store.\n- Existing named consumers continue to read `:yin/*` datoms and reconstruct/query the named AST.\n\nProjected persistence should be separate:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.debruijn/fingerprint fp\n :yin.debruijn/datoms      (projected->datoms projection)}\n```\n\nThis canonical envelope is stored through a separate DaoJing content-store handle. It must never be appended to the named AST medium or passed to named consumers.\n\n## 3. Dedupe rule\n\nUse existing DaoJing write-idempotent storage, not a pre-write lookup.\n\n`dao.jing/materialize!` already:\n\n- derives a content address;\n- accepts `:inserted` or `:present`;\n- verifies read-back content;\n- never overwrites.\n\nThe projected envelope contains the Merkle fingerprint and canonical records. D3 guarantees that equal fingerprints imply equal canonical records, so equal projections produce equal envelopes and naturally deduplicate through `materialize!`. The physical DaoJing address is distinct from the semantic Merkle fingerprint; return both.\n\nDo not deduplicate named artifacts by fingerprint. Named storage preserves provenance, names, and queryable history.\n\n## 4. Bounded D5 file list\n\nAllowed:\n\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc` — new explicit post-emission orchestration function and projected-envelope persistence shape.\n- `test/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc` — tests for named/projected separation, projection failure isolation, projected write failure, idempotent dedupe, and complete-batch ordering.\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc` — only if a small pure projected-envelope constructor is needed; existing projection and storage functions should otherwise remain unchanged.\n- One explicitly selected composition caller, only when it already owns both a named writer and projected DaoJing store. No current Yang compiler or REPL caller satisfies that boundary, so D5 should not force a REPL edit.\n\nForbidden:\n\n- modifying `yang.*` compiler semantics;\n- modifying `yin.vm` emission or AST schema;\n- modifying `yin.vm.encoder`, the semantic VM, linearization, named consumers, `dao.stream`, `dao.lease`, or the waitset;\n- mixing projected records into the named stream;\n- making named persistence contingent on projection success.\n\n## 5. Ordering hazard and resolution\n\nProjection must observe one complete root-framed named graph, never a mid-emission prefix. The emitter’s `:yin/root` marker is the frame boundary and is emitted last. The orchestration must therefore:\n\n- materialize one complete named batch;\n- never project individual datoms as they are emitted;\n- reset framing/index state per batch;\n- reject a partial graph or missing root;\n- avoid appending projected records until the named batch has completed successfully.\n\nThis preserves atomic named visibility and prevents projection from observing an incomplete AST."}}
